<%# The engine's layout is the host's layout with the engine's own things
    added: its stylesheet, its destination, and the grid its pages are laid
    out on. Everything else — the shell, the mark, the door — is the host's,
    which is what makes this a tool inside an application rather than an
    application of its own.

    The host's layout is expected to render its-swiss's shell and to place
    :sections in its masthead nav; the dummy application under test/ shows
    the least a host has to do. %>

<% content_for :head do %>
  <%# Unlayered, like a host's own, so it wins over the library wherever the
      two meet, whatever order they arrive in. %>
  <%= stylesheet_link_tag "projects/components", "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
<% end %>

<% content_for :main_class, "grid" %>

<%# No :sections. The host's subnav already carries the tool's mark and its
    name, and this tool has one destination — a second link reading "Projects"
    under a mark reading "Projects" is the same word twice. A tool fills that
    slot when it has surfaces to name, and this one does not. %>

<%= render template: "layouts/application" %>
